  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 252 PM MDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nIt is mostly sunny across the low elevations of Colorado, and \nconsequently, much warmer that it was around this time yesterday \nwith temperature readings at Denver International Airport in the \nlow 90s. Cloud cover is increasing over the mountains, and a few \nshowers have already developed over the Park and Front Range. Weak\nupper level ridging moving onto the Northern Plains has begun to \nturn flow aloft a tad more southwesterly, which will help advect \nmonsoonal moisture into the region. For the rest of the afternoon \nand evening, scattered showers and thunderstorms will be able to \nproduce gusty winds up to 40-50 mph. The best dry microburst \npotential will be over the Denver metro and I-25 corridor, where \nhot afternoon temperatures (low 90s) and surface dewpoints in the \n40s will yield DCAPE values 1200-1500 J/kg. Although we expect \nshower activity over the plains to cease by around 9PM this \nevening, multiple rounds of showers will continue over the \nmountains in the overnight hours as mid-level moisture continues \nto increase. A stray shower over the Denver metro cannot be ruled \nout Monday morning. However, we do not expect this rainfall to \namount to more than a trace. \n\nAn upper-level trough will track through Montana through the \nday on Monday, flattening out as it progresses east, and in turn \nshifting upper-level flow to the west-southwest. Near cross-barrier \nflow will make for a breezy day in the mountains, with sporadic \ngusts up to 50 mph over the highest peaks and ridges. Weak \ndownsloping and daytime mixing may also lead to periods of gusty \nwesterly winds (20-30 mph) for the foothills and western Denver \nmetro. Another round of showers will be possible over the east \nColorado plains Monday afternoon, between 2pm and 7pm. \n\nUncertainty remains regarding how the synoptic pattern will evolve \nthis week, and thus, how the rainfall chances will play out each \nday. A cold front will move through the region in the very early \nmorning hours of Tuesday, bringing cooler air and a layer of stratus \nthat will likely persist through most of the day. Temperatures on \nTuesday will be cooler than normal, with afternoon highs only \nreaching into the 70s at low elevations, and 50s-60s for the \nmountains. Moisture Tuesday afternoon now looks less promising than \nit did yesterday due to slightly more zonal mid-level flow. However, \nthere will likely still be enough moisture around for scattered \nshowers over the high terrain and some isolated showers over the \nplains. \n\nWeak ridging returns on Wednesday and temperatures will be back to \nnear-seasonal. Although warmer temperatures will lead to better \ninstability for convective development, questions remain\nregarding how much moisture will be available for the plains. \nTherefore, the better shower chances will likely reside over the \nmountains and Palmer Divide. Daily chances for precipitation now \nappear to continue through the weekend. However, there is \nconsiderable uncertainty regarding the development and timing of a\nfew shortwaves Friday through Sunday. Long-range ensembles are \ntrending drier for next week, although precipitable water means \nwill remain near the climatological average.",
